#
# three-column-gate — do the Rust, C and Python columns agree?
#
# One command, one verdict, over the whole interop corpus in every
# configuration, in both directions.
#
#   tools/wireprobe/three-column-gate            the fast tier
#   tools/wireprobe/three-column-gate --full     every column, every case
#
# The gate itself is `crates/wire-probe/tests/three_column_gate.rs`; this
# builds what the three columns need and runs it with the tier set. The
# test is also what `cargo test --workspace` runs, in the fast tier, so
# the two are the same gate and not two.
#
# ## The tiers
#
#   fast   the Rust and Python columns over every case; the C column over
#          one case per construct family plus every case a column has been
#          measured disagreeing on. About a minute and a half warm on an
#          M1. What it leaves out is named in `representative` in the
#          test and repeated in the fixture the run writes.
#
#   full   the same with the C column over every case. About 24 minutes
#          the first time on an M1 and about a minute after that, because
#          compiled probes are kept in target/wireprobe-c-cache and only
#          a probe that is not there is built.
#
#          The first time is long on macOS for a reason that has nothing
#          to do with this repository: the system validates the signature
#          of every freshly linked binary on its first execution, about
#          235 ms each, and the check is serialised across processes —
#          40 fresh binaries take the same 9.1 s whether they run one at
#          a time or ten at a time. The matrix is 5,500 distinct
#          binaries. On Linux there is no such check.
#
# ## One gate at a time
#
# The gate takes an exclusive lock, target/three-column-gate.lock, for
# the whole run. A second gate in the same checkout stops immediately
# and names the process holding it — it does not wait, and it does not
# report.
#
# That is not politeness about CPU. The intermediates and the report are
# fixed paths under target/, and two runs sharing them interleave into
# one file: the reader gets some rows from its own run and some from the
# other's, and the mixture parses. A gate that can quietly report a
# result mixed out of two runs is not a gate, and this one was caught
# doing it twice on 2026-08-10.
#
# A lock left behind by a run that was killed is cleared automatically —
# the holder's pid is checked — and the takeover says so.
#
# ## What it writes
#
#   crates/wire-probe/tests/fixtures/three_column_matrix.md
#
# the matrix per column, per direction and per configuration, every
# disagreement, and what the run did not cover. Written through a
# temporary and renamed into place, so a killed run never leaves half a
# report behind.
set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
root="$(cd "$here/../.." && pwd)"
tier=fast

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --full) tier=full; shift ;;
        --fast) tier=fast; shift ;;
        --help|-h) sed -n '5,40p' "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; exit 0 ;;
        *) echo "three-column-gate: \`$1\` is not an option" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
done

# The two foreign columns are driven by programs, and a gate that built
# them lazily inside the test would charge the build to the first
# column's stopwatch and report it as that column's cost.
echo "three-column-gate: building the C and Python matrix drivers" >&2
cargo build -q --manifest-path "$root/Cargo.toml" --release \
    -p zerodds-wireprobe-c-gen --bin wireprobe-c-matrix
cargo build -q --manifest-path "$root/Cargo.toml" --release \
    -p zerodds-wireprobe-python-gen --bin wireprobe-python-matrix

echo "three-column-gate: tier $tier" >&2
ZERODDS_GATE_TIER="$tier" cargo test --manifest-path "$root/Cargo.toml" \
    -p zerodds-wire-probe --test three_column_gate -- --nocapture
